\DOC LOCAL_EPDOUBLE_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_EPDOUBLE_TAC : int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually} machine-execution goal across a called point-doubling
subroutine, replacing the assumptions about the current machine state {sn} by
corresponding ones for a successor state, plus a summary theorem about the
subroutine's result.

\DESCRIBE
The single {int} argument {n} fixes the index of the current state in the
assumptions: the state variable {sn} is matched and a new state {s(n+1)} is
introduced, with the goal's conclusion restated about it. Exactly one subgoal
results. Invariant-style assumptions (size/nonoverlap conditions, numeric
bounds, previously derived {forall}-facts about earlier results) are kept
unchanged. All assumptions mentioning the old state — register and memory
reads, flag equations, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame relating the
initial state to the old one — are removed and re-added with the new state
substituted, except that the flag assumptions about the old state are dropped,
the program-counter assumption is replaced by one for a new address, and the
{MAYCHANGE} frame gains additional components covering the memory region
written by the subroutine. A new universally quantified assumption is added
stating the doubling specification for the output buffer read in the new state.
{
   A[sn] ?- eventually ... sn
   ========================================  LOCAL_EPDOUBLE_TAC n
   A'[s(n+1)], (!z1 ... zp. doubling spec for output buffer read in s(n+1)) ?- eventually ... s(n+1)
}

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this single kind of transformation, on goals of this
one shape.

\ENDDOC
